I've read the full test file (MSTest 4.1 / .NET 10) and the extended catalog. Here is the full 19-smell audit.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 1           | (class-level)  |
| **Total**| **9**       | **8 of 9 methods** |

One test (`ValidateOrder_NullOrder_ThrowsArgumentNullException`) is **clean** — it uses `Assert.ThrowsException<T>`, asserts on a specific property, and has clear intent. It is the model to follow.

---

## Findings by Severity

### ▸ HIGH

---

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

Only one branch executes per run — the other is never verified. This hides a gap.

**Fix — split into two deterministic tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardOrder_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumOrder_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 5) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// ← no assertion; passes even if the method returns garbage
```

This provides false confidence — the test passes as long as no exception is thrown, regardless of behavior.

**Fix — assert the expected outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_ValidOrder_ReturnsProcessedResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);
Thread.Sleep(2000);  // ← arbitrary 2-second wait
Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
```

Non-deterministic: passes on fast machines, flakes in CI. Adds 2 seconds to every suite run.

**Fix — await the async operation or poll with a timeout:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
